    "content": "The Space Shuttle Challenger disaster occurred on January 28, 1986, when the orbiter broke apart 73 seconds into its 25th flight (STS-51L) at approximately 46,000 feet, killing all seven crew members. The failure was caused by O-ring seals in the right solid rocket booster failing due to cold weather, resulting in a catastrophic explosion.This video shows the launch and the immediate aftermath of the Challenger disaster:05:09NBCLAYouTube• Jan 28, 2026Key Details of the Challenger Disaster:Date: January 28, 1986, at 11:39 a.m. EST.Crew: The seven crew members were Commander Dick Scobee, Pilot Michael Smith, Mission Specialists Ronald McNair, Ellison Onizuka, and Judith Resnik, and Payload Specialists Gregory Jarvis and Christa McAuliffe (the first teacher in space).Cause: Cold temperatures at launch caused O-ring seals in the right Solid Rocket Booster (SRB) to fail, allowing hot gas to breach the external fuel tank, causing a structural failure.Fate: The shuttle disintegrated, with debris falling into the Atlantic Ocean off the coast of Cape Canaveral, Florida.Speed: At the time of the explosion, 73 seconds into the flight, the shuttle was moving at nearly twice the speed of sound.Aftermath: The shuttle program was grounded for over two years, and the disaster led to major safety, management, and technical changes at NASA.The crew is honored through the Challenger Center for Space Science Education, founded by the families of the crew to promote STEM education.",
